**Theorem 3.2 (Fifteen signs, four scalar inequalities and one cubic).**
The following three conditions are equivalent:

(a) $\mathcal B_{C,y,\delta}(a)\succeq0$ for every real
$a\in\mathbb R^5$.

(b) $\mathcal R(p)\succeq0$ and $\mathcal T(p)\succeq0$
for every $p\in\Delta_2$.

(c) All of the following hold:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\alpha_{ik}\ge0\quad(i,k\in\{0,+,-\}),\qquad
\beta_{jk}\ge0\quad(j\in\{+,-\},\ k\in\{0,+,-\}),\\
|c_{ij}|\le\sqrt{\alpha_{ii}\alpha_{jj}}
                +\sqrt{\alpha_{ij}\alpha_{ji}}
\quad((i,j)=(0,+),(0,-),(+,-)),\\
|t|\le\sqrt{\beta_{++}\beta_{--}}
                +\sqrt{\beta_{+-}\beta_{-+}},\\
\mathscr F(p)\ge0\quad\text{whenever }p_i>0,\quad\sum_ip_i=1.
\end{gathered}
\tag{3.5}
$$

The first line contains nine plus six coefficient signs. The next
two lines contain exactly four scalar inequalities. Those finite
conditions already imply $\mathscr F\ge0$ on the whole boundary
of $\Delta_2$. They do not in general imply its interior
nonnegativity.

Proof. The scalar form of (3.1) is

$$
b^T\mathcal B_{C,y,\delta}(a)b
=\|a\|^2y^Th(b)-\langle C,h(a)h(b)^T\rangle
                  -\frac12\sum_\nu\delta_\nu z_\nu(a)^Tz_\nu(b),
\tag{3.6}
$$

where $\langle C,M\rangle=\sum_{i,j}C_{ij}M_{ij}$.
It is invariant under simultaneous $g\in\mathcal G$ on $a,b$,
by the proof of (2.3). Consequently
$\mathcal B(ga)=g\mathcal B(a)g^T$. For nonzero $a$, first
divide by $\|a\|$ and use the canonical preparation construction
of Theorem 2.2. Orthogonal congruence and the positive factor
$\|a\|^2$ preserve positivity. The zero vector gives the zero
matrix and requires no separate assumption.

At the canonical preparation, the complete list of plane coordinates is

$$
\begin{aligned}
z_+(a(p))&=(\sqrt{2p_0p_+},0),&
z_-(a(p))&=(\sqrt{2p_0p_-},0),\\
z_{2+}(a(p))&=(p_+/\sqrt2,0),&
z_{2-}(a(p))&=(p_-/\sqrt2,0),\\
z_\Delta(a(p))&=(\sqrt{p_+p_-},0),&
z_\Sigma(a(p))&=(\sqrt{p_+p_-},0).
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.7}
$$

Substitution into the literal normalized matrices (1.6) gives

$$
\mathcal B_{C,y,\delta}(a(p))=
\mathcal R(p)\oplus\mathcal T(p)
\tag{3.8}
$$

in the reordered adapted basis. In particular the radial mixed entry
contains $\delta_\Delta+\delta_\Sigma$, while the tangent mixed
entry contains $\delta_\Delta-\delta_\Sigma$. This proves (a)
equivalent to (b), without omitting either mixed plane.

The diagonal entries of (3.8) are five linear forms on the nonnegative
orthant. Their nonnegativity on the simplex is equivalent to
nonnegativity of their fifteen coefficients, by testing the three
vertices. This proves the first line of (3.5) in both directions.

For a radial pair $i,j$, its principal minor is

$$
A_i(p)A_j(p)-c_{ij}^2p_ip_j.
\tag{3.9}
$$

On the face where the third population vanishes, write $x=p_i,y=p_j$.
The expression becomes $ax^2+bxy+cy^2$, with

$$
a=\alpha_{ii}\alpha_{ji},\qquad
b=\alpha_{ii}\alpha_{jj}+\alpha_{ij}\alpha_{ji}-c_{ij}^2,
\qquad c=\alpha_{ij}\alpha_{jj}.
\tag{3.10}
$$

Here $a,c\ge0$. The elementary binary quadratic criterion on the
nonnegative orthant is $b+2\sqrt{ac}\ge0$. For sufficiency,
$ax^2+cy^2\ge2\sqrt{ac}xy$. For necessity when $a,c>0$, take
$(x,y)=(\sqrt c,\sqrt a)$. If $a=0,c>0$ and $b<0$, take
$y=1$ and $x$ sufficiently large; if $c=0,a>0$ reverse the roles.
If both vanish, take $x=y=1$. Thus the criterion includes all
degenerate coefficients. This is a standard binary quadratic
calculation, used here inside the full three-sector criterion.

Using (3.10), it is exactly

$$
c_{ij}^2\le
\left(\sqrt{\alpha_{ii}\alpha_{jj}}
           +\sqrt{\alpha_{ij}\alpha_{ji}}\right)^2,
\tag{3.11}
$$

equivalent to the corresponding inequality in (3.5). Adding the
third nonnegative population can only increase each of $A_i,A_j$,
because all their coefficients are nonnegative. Both factors are
already nonnegative, and the subtracted term in (3.9) is unchanged.
Hence the face criterion is equivalent to nonnegativity of this
minor on the entire orthant. Homogeneity justifies using arbitrary
$(x,y)\ge0$ rather than only their normalized face.

The identical binary calculation for
$B_+(p)B_-(p)-t^2p_+p_-$, with $p_0$ as the third population,
gives exactly the fourth scalar inequality. Thus the finite
conditions in (3.5) are equivalent to all one-by-one and two-by-two
principal minors of both blocks being nonnegative for every $p$.

The only remaining principal minor is $\det\mathcal R(p)$.
Expanding its determinant gives exactly $\mathscr F(p)$ in (3.4).
The product of its three negative off-diagonal entries contributes
$-2uvw\,p_0p_+p_-$; the sign of $uvw$ is not discarded.
If one population is zero, that row's off-diagonal entries vanish.
The radial block is then a nonnegative one-by-one block and the
other two-by-two principal block. Its determinant is nonnegative
by the conditions already proved. This includes vertices and
multiple zero populations. For all strictly positive populations,
homogeneity reduces the remaining determinant condition to the
last line of (3.5).

The standard principal-minor criterion for a real symmetric matrix
says that all its principal minors are nonnegative exactly when it
is positive semidefinite. Applied to these blocks of sizes three
and two, it finishes (b) equivalent to (c). No strictly positive
pivot, invertible principal submatrix, or division by a population
was used. $\square$

